 2011&#93;</span>. （原始內容<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20121103165903/http://www.boston.com/yourtown/concord/articles/2011/06/09/lexington_author_reading_in_westborough/\">存檔</a>於2012-11-03）.</cite><span title=\"ctx&#95;ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rfr&#95;id=info%3Asid%2Fzh.wikipedia.org%3A%E4%BB%96%E6%98%AF%E4%B8%80%E4%B8%AA%E5%BF%AB%E4%B9%90%E7%9A%84%E5%A5%BD%E5%B0%8F%E4%BC%99&amp;rft.atitle=Mining+nuggets+of+music+history&amp;rft.aufirst=Nancy+Shohet&amp;rft.aulast=West&amp;rft.date=2011-06-09&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=The+Boston+Globe&amp;rft&#95;id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.boston.com%2Fyourtown%2Fconcord%2Farticles%2F2011%2F06%2F09%2Flexington&#95;author&#95;reading&#95;in&#95;westborough%2F&amp;rft&#95;val&#95;fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal\" class=\"Z3988\"><span style=\"display:none;\">&#160;</span></span></span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-Cryer-4\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-Cryer_4-0\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\"><link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r95173179\" /><cite id=\"CITEREFCryer2010\" class=\"citation book\"><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=Max_Cryer&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Max Cryer（頁面不存在）\">Cryer, Max</a>. <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://books.google.com/books?id=E0QfmrqgbHwC\">Love Me Tender: The Stories Behind the World's Favourite Songs</a>. Exile Publishing. 2010: 26 ff. <a href=\"/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-1-4587-7956-4\" title=\"Special:BookSources/978-1-4587-7956-4\"><span title=\"國際標準書號\">ISBN</span>&#160;978-1-4587-7956-4</a>.</cite><span title=\"ctx&#95;ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rfr&#95;id=info%3Asid%2Fzh.wikipedia.org%3A%E4%BB%96%E6%98%AF%E4%B8%80%E4%B8%AA%E5%BF%AB%E4%B9%90%E7%9A%84%E5%A5%BD%E5%B0%8F%E4%BC%99&amp;rft.aufirst=Max&amp;rft.aulast=Cryer&amp;rft.btitle=Love+Me+Tender%3A+The+Stories+Behind+the+World%27s+Favourite+Songs&amp;rft.date=2010&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.isbn=978-1-4587-7956-4&amp;rft.pages=26+ff&amp;rft.pub=Exile+Publishing&amp;rft&#95;id=https%3A%2F%2Fbooks.google.com%2Fbooks%3Fid%3DE0QfmrqgbHwC&amp;rft&#95;val&#95;fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook\" class=\"Z3988\"><span style=\"display:none;\">&#160;</span></span></span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-5\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-5\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\"><i><a href=\"/wiki/%E6%B3%B0%E6%99%A4%E5%A3%AB%E6%8A%A5\" title=\"泰晤士報\">The Times</a></i> (London, England), 28 March 1826, p. 2: \"The Power of Music: A visiting foreigner, trying to recall the address of his lodgings in Marlborough Street, hums the tune to a London cabman: he immediately recognises it as 'Malbrook<span style=\"padding-right:.2em;\">'</span>\".</span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-6\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-6\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">The song may have featured in an \"extravaganza\" given at the Princess Theatre in London at Easter 1846, during which fairies hold a moonlight meeting: \"...the meeting closes with a song of thanks to Robin Goodfellow (Miss Marshall), who had occupied the chair, ...and who is assured that 'he's a jolly good fellow'.\" \"Princess's.\" <i><a href=\"/wiki/%E6%B3%B0%E6%99%A4%E5%A3%AB%E6%8A%A5\" title=\"泰晤士報\">The Times</a></i> (London, England) 14 April 1846: 5. <i>The Times</i> Digital Archive. Web. 1 October 2012.<sup class=\"noprint Template-Fact\"><a href=\"/wiki/Wikipedia:%E5%88%97%E6%98%8E%E6%9D%A5%E6%BA%90\" title=\"Wikipedia:列明來源\"><span style=\"white-space: nowrap;\" title=\"需要完整來源。\">&#91;需要完整來源&#93;</span></a></sup></span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-7\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-7\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\"><i><a href=\"/wiki/%E6%B3%B0%E6%99%A4%E5%A3%AB%E6%8A%A5\" title=\"泰晤士報\">The Times</a></i> reprinted an article from <i><a href=\"/wiki/%E7%AC%A8%E6%8B%99%E9%9B%9C%E8%AA%8C\" title=\"笨拙雜誌\">Punch</a></i> describing a drunken speech given at a (fictional) public meeting. The speech ends: \"Zshenl'men, here's all your vehgood healts! I beggapard'n – here's my honangal'n fren's shjolly goo' health! 'For he's a jolly good fellow', &amp;c (Chorus by the whole of the company, amid which the right hon. orator tumbled down.)\" \"The After Dinner Speech at the Improvement Club\". <i>The Times</i>, (London, England) 23 March 1854: 10. <i>The Times</i> Digital Archive. Web. 1 October 2012.<sup class=\"noprint Template-Fact\"><a href=\"/wiki/Wikipedia:%E5%88%97%E6%98%8E%E6%9D%A5%E6%BA%90\" title=\"Wikipedia:列明來源\"><span style=\"white-space: nowrap;\" title=\"需要完整來源。\">&#91;需要完整來源&#93;</span></a></sup></span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-8\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-8\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">Review of a piano recital: \"As a finale he performed for the first time, a burlesque on the French air, 'Marlbrook', better known to the American student of harmony as 'He's a jolly good fellow'.\" <i><a href=\"/wiki/The_New_York_Times\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"The New York Times\">The New York Times</a></i>, 4 October 1862</span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-9\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-9\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">An 1859 version quoted in <i>The Times</i>, however, has some \"red-faced\" English officers at an Indian entertainment dancing before their host: \"...declaring that he was 'a right good fellow; he's a jolly good fellow, which nobody dare deny hip, hip, hip, hoorah!' &amp;c.\" <i><a href=\"/wiki/The_Times\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"The Times\">The Times</a></i> (London, England), 24 March 1859, p. 9</span>\n</li>\n</ol></div>\n<div class=\"navbox-styles\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r95173189\">.mw-parser-output .hlist dl,.mw-parser-output .hlist ol,.mw-parser-output .hlist ul{mar